Why GPL rather than MIT

MIT lets anyone take this, close it, and sell it without giving anything back.
GPL does not: anyone distributing a modified version has to publish their source
under the same terms. That is what keeps it open rather than merely free today.

The dependencies allow it. CLAP, Dear ImGui and RtMidi are all permissively
licensed and can be combined into a GPL work. Steinberg's VST3 SDK, which the
optional VST3 build links against, is itself dual-licensed GPLv3 or proprietary -
so a GPLv3 LumiPaint can ship as a VST3, while an MIT one arguably could not have
without taking Steinberg's proprietary terms.

One consequence worth understanding before you commit: you cannot later sell a
closed version of this yourself without the agreement of everyone who has
contributed by then. While you are the only author that costs you nothing, since
a sole copyright holder can always relicense their own work. It stops being free
the moment you accept someone else's patch.
